 bounds on the size of certain graphs defined by specific rigidity properties. In this section we collect some corollaries of this type, including an affirmative answer to a special case of a recent conjecture of the second author.\n\nAn \\(\\mathcal{R}_d\\)-connected graph G is called minimally \\(\\mathcal{R}_d\\)-connected, if \\(G-e\\) is not \\(\\mathcal{R}_d\\)-connected for all \\(e\\in E(G)\\). The cases \\(d=1,2\\) of the following conjecture were settled in [13].\n\n### Conjecture 8.1\n\n[13] Let \\(G=(V,E)\\) be a minimally \\(\\mathcal{R}_d\\)-connected graph. Then we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} |E|\\le (d+1)|V|-\\left( {\\begin{array}{c}d+2\\\\ 2\\end{array}}\\right) , \\end{aligned}$$\n\n(20)\n\nwhere equality holds if and only if \\(G=K_{d+2}\\).\n\nBy using our results on circuit decompositions of matroids, we can verify the \\(d=3\\) case of Conjecture 8.1 in a stronger form. We need the following lemma.\n\n### Lemma 8.2\n\n[13] Let \\(G=(V,E)\\) be a minimally \\(\\mathcal{R}_d\\)-connected graph and let \\((C_1, C_2,\\ldots , C_{t})\\) be an ear-decomposition. Then \\(D_j\\) induces a minimally \\(\\mathcal{R}_d\\)-connected subgraph for all \\(1\\le j\\le t\\).\n\nThe main result of this section is as follows.\n\n### Theorem 8.3\n\nLet \\(G=(V,E)\\) be a minimally \\(\\mathcal{R}_d\\)-connected graph for some \\(d\\ge 3\\). Then we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} |E| \\le \\frac{1}{2} (3d - 1) |V| - \\left( {\\begin{array}{c}d + 2\\\\ 2\\end{array}}\\right) - \\frac{1}{2} (d - 3) (d + 2)-2k_d(G) \\end{aligned}$$\n\n(21)\n\nwith equality if and only if \\(G=K_{d+2}\\).\n\n### Proof\n\nLet \\((C_1,C_2,\\dots ,C_{t_1},C_{t_1+1},\\dots , C_t)\\) be an ear-decomposition of \\(\\mathcal{R}_d(G)\\). We may assume^{Footnote 2} that the lobes of the first \\(t_1\\) ears add new vertices and the remaining \\(t-t_1\\) lobes add no vertices. Let H be the spanning subgraph of G induced by \\(D_{t_1}\\). We claim that \\(|\\tilde{C}_i|\\ge 2\\) for all \\(t_1+1\\le i\\le t\\). To see this suppose that \\(|C_i|=\\{e\\}\\) for some \\(t_1+1\\le i\\le t\\) and \\(e\\in E\\). Then the subgraphs induced by \\(D_i\\) and \\(D_i-e=D_{i-1}\\) are both \\(\\mathcal{R}_d\\)-connected, contradicting Lemma 8.2. Thus \\(t-t_1\\le \\frac{|E|-|E(H)|}{2}\\). Since \\(V(H)=V(G)\\), we can deduce that\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} k_d(H)-k_d(G)=|E|-|E(H)|-(t-t_1)\\ge \\frac{|E|-|E(H)|}{2}. \\end{aligned}$$\n\n(22)\n\nWe can now use (22), (15), (17), and the inequality \\(r_d^*(H)\\ge 1\\) to obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned} |E|&\\le |E(H)| + 2(k_d(H)-k_d(G)) = d|V| - \\left( {\\begin{array}{c}d + 1\\\\ 2\\end{array}}\\right) +k_d(H)+r^*_d(H)-2k_d(G)\\\\&\\le \\frac{1}{2} (3d - 1) |V| - \\left( {\\begin{array}{c}d + 1\\\\ 2\\end{array}}\\right) - \\frac{1}{2} (d - 1) (r_d^*(H) + d+1)+ r_d^*(H) -2k_d(G)\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2} (3d - 1) |V| - \\left( {\\begin{array}{c}d + 2\\\\ 2\\end{array}}\\right) - \\frac{1}{2} (d - 3) (r_d^*(H) + d + 1)-2k_d(G)\\\\&\\le \\frac{1}{2} (3d - 1) |V| - \\left( {\\begin{array}{c}d + 2\\\\ 2\\end{array}}\\right) - \\frac{1}{2} (d - 3) (d + 2)-2k_d(G). \\end{aligned}$$\n\nIt also follows that equality holds in (21) only if H is an \\(\\mathcal{R}_d\\)-circuit that achieves equality in (17). In that case H, and hence also G, must be a \\(K_{d+2}\\) by Lemma 5.2. \\(\\square \\)\n\nTheorem 8.3 improves on the upper bound given for the size of a minimally \\(\\mathcal{R}_d\\)-connected graph in [13] and implies an affirmative answer to the \\(d=3\\) case of Conjecture 8.1.\n\n## Acknowledgements\n\nWe thank the referees for carefully reading our paper and providing several valuable comments.\n\nThe second author was supported by the MTA-ELTE Momentum Matroid Optimization Research Group and the National Research, Development and Innovation Fund of Hungary, financed under the ELTE TKP 2021-NKTA-62 funding scheme.\n\n## Funding\n\nOpen access funding provided by Eötvös Loránd University.\n\n## Author information\n\n### Authors and Affiliati